.\" Manual page for p25d; the full description is docs/p25d.md.
.\" Source: src/p25d.c
.TH "P25D" "8" "November 22, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
p25d \- decode APCO Project 25 (P25) channels from radiod and follow trunked calls
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p25d
.RI "[options] [-I input ...] [-R output] [-F frames] [input ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
p25d listens to one or more multicast groups carrying complex baseband (I/Q)
channels from radiod and decodes every P25 channel on them, each in its own
thread. It decodes and logs every trunking message, header, link control word,
encryption sync and packet data unit. When a control channel grants a voice
channel, p25d asks radiod for that channel, decodes it, and lets it go when
the hang time after the call's end has passed with no other call granted
there. Phase 1 (FDMA) control, voice and conventional channels are
decoded, and the Phase 2 (TDMA) control and voice channels of trunked systems,
both timeslots of a carrier from one radiod channel.
.PP
The speech of clear (unencrypted) calls can be sent as an 8 kHz RTP audio
stream that monitor plays and pcmrecord records (when p25d is built with
mbelib), and the vocoder frames
themselves can be sent as an RTP stream for a vocoder of your own. The receive
filter is chosen for each channel from its signal and needs no setting.
.PP
This page is a summary. The package's docs/p25d.md describes the radiod
configuration, call following, the encryption rule, the frame packet format
and the log lines in full.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-I group, \-\-input group"
Add an input group. May be repeated; positional groups are also accepted.
.TP
.B "\-R group, \-\-pcm\-out group"
Send decoded speech to this multicast group. Without it no audio is sent.
Needs a p25d built with mbelib.
.TP
.B "\-F group, \-\-frames\-out group"
Send the vocoder frames (IMBE, AMBE+2) to this multicast group. Works without
mbelib.
.TP
.B "\-E, \-\-frames\-encrypted"
With \-F, also send the frames of encrypted voice, flagged as encrypted, with
their encryption parameters. p25d never decodes them.
.TP
.B "\-N name, \-\-name name"
Name the voice output is advertised under. Default p25d; the frame output is
advertised as "name frames".
.TP
.B "\-T ttl, \-\-ttl ttl"
Multicast TTL of the voice and frame output. Default 0: this host only.
.TP
.B "\-p tos, \-\-tos tos"
IP type of service of the voice and frame output. Default 184 (expedited
forwarding).
.TP
.B "\-n, \-\-no\-follow"
Do not follow calls; decode only the channels already there.
.TP
.B "\-g list, \-\-talkgroups list"
Follow group calls on only these talkgroups, for example 2501,2600\-2699. May
be repeated. This and \-x choose which trunked calls to follow; they do not
filter conventional channels.
.TP
.B "\-x list, \-\-exclude list"
Never follow these talkgroups. May be repeated.
.TP
.B "\-m n, \-\-max\-calls n"
Follow at most n calls at once. Default: no limit.
.TP
.B "\-e, \-\-encrypted"
Follow encrypted calls too. They are logged; their voice is never decoded.
.TP
.B "\-H seconds, \-\-hangtime seconds"
Keep a voice channel, and its receiver, this long after the last call on it
ends, so that a call granted there again finds it running. Default 30;
fractions are allowed; 0 releases the channel as soon as its last call ends;
at most 86400.
The clock starts at the end of the last call on the channel; for 3 s after a
call it followed (or the hang time, if shorter) a grant update naming the same
call there starts no new call, while a grant, voice, or an update naming
another talkgroup does: a reply announced only by updates is followed from its
own voice. The
default comes from a survey of four trunked sites: 3 s catches every
deliberate reuse (systems' own hang and replies) and saves about half of all
channel requests for the least load; 30 s saves 64 to 84% on busy systems for
two to three idle receivers per site on average, the peak being bounded by the
site's voice carriers. Quiet systems gain nothing past 3 s. A Phase 2
channel is kept only when neither timeslot has a call. \-m counts calls, not
kept channels; there is at most one kept channel per voice frequency granted in
the hang time, and at most 64 voice channels per control channel in all. Kept
channels are released at once when p25d exits or the control channel goes
away. With \-v, keeping, reusing and releasing a channel are logged.
.TP
.B "\-S ssrc, \-\-ssrc ssrc"
Decode only this channel. Turns call following off.
.TP
.B "\-o source, \-\-source source"
Accept only this sender, by name or address (source-specific multicast).
.TP
.B "\-A interface, \-\-iface interface"
Set default multicast interface.
.TP
.B "\-t seconds, \-\-timeout seconds"
Close a channel after this long without data. Default 20.
.TP
.B "\-u seconds, \-\-update seconds"
Interval between the status lines that \-v turns on. Default 10.
.TP
.B "\-l locale, \-\-locale locale"
Locale for number formatting. Default $LANG.
.TP
.B "\-v, \-\-verbose"
Increase diagnostics; repeat for more. At \-v each channel also logs a status
line.
.TP
.B "\-V, \-\-version"
Print version and exit.
.SH RADIOD CONFIGURATION
p25d needs I/Q, not demodulated audio, at 19.2 kHz (four samples per 4800 baud
symbol), with the AGC off and a fixed gain. For a trunked system configure
only the control channel (for a site that moves its control channel, every
frequency it can move to: see below); for a conventional channel configure it
the same way. An example is in config/examples/fragments/p25.conf:
.PP
.nf
[P25]
data = p25\-iq.local
mode = iq
samprate = 19200
low = \-5k
high = +5k
encoding = f32le
agc = no
gain = 60
freq = "857m98125"
.fi
.PP
A Phase 2 (TDMA) control channel is configured the same way but at 24 kHz
("samprate = 24000", four samples per 6000 baud symbol). No option says which
kind a channel is: on a channel at 24 kHz or more that it did not ask for,
p25d runs its Phase 1 and Phase 2 receivers side by side until one of them
decodes something that passes its check, and keeps that one. A call on the
other timeslot of a TDMA control channel's own carrier is decoded from the
control channel itself; nothing more is asked of radiod.
.PP
Voice channels are asked for in the control channel's form, except that a
Phase 2 voice channel is always asked for at 24 kHz, and a Phase 1 voice
channel granted on a Phase 2 control channel at 19.2 kHz. They need no
configuration, except that radiod builds them from its [global] section: see
NOTES. A voice channel is asked for with a lifetime of 5 seconds, so one
p25d stops asking for goes away by itself. While a call uses it, and while it
is kept after one, p25d refreshes it every half second (a command goes to
radiod only when a third of the lifetime has passed). It is released when the
hang time (\-H) after its last call has passed, or at once when p25d exits; no
release is sent for a channel whose lifetime has already run out. A trunking
block heard on a voice channel is acted on but is not taken as a sign that the
control channel is being received; a protected block's fields are not used; a
multi-block trunking packet whose data blocks fail their CRC is ignored.
.PP
p25d knows a channel by its SSRC and the address and port it comes from, and
ties a voice channel to its control channel by the radiod command address both
name in their status. When two systems served by one radiod use the same voice
frequency, the channel belongs to the site that asked for it last.
.PP
Control channel rotation. Some sites move their control channel among a set
of frequencies, which they announce as secondary control channels (logged
with \-v as "trunk control secondary"). Configure every one of them in radiod
in the control channel's form. A channel takes over the site's control role
when an RFSS status broadcast decoded there names the same system, RFSS and
site with that channel's frequency, and the present control channel has been
silent for 2 seconds; the site keeps its calls and voice channels, the old
control channel becomes an ordinary channel that may be granted as a voice
channel, and a line says "control channel moved to". Meanwhile the new
channel's trunking goes to the site as trunking heard on a voice channel does.
p25d does not move to a channel it asked radiod for itself, and says to
configure it. A call granted on the old control channel's frequency before the
move loses its first voice units until the move. A later version is to ask
radiod for the announced secondary control channels when the control channel
goes silent, so that only one need be configured.
.SH INPUT AND OUTPUT
Decoded events go to standard output, one line each, starting with the time of
the signal, the SSRC and the channel frequency; errors and startup messages go
to standard error.
.PP
The voice stream (\-R) is 8 kHz mono 16-bit audio, one RTP packet per 20 ms,
with status packets as radiod sends them. A trunked group call's stream has
the talkgroup number as its SSRC; any other call, and a conventional channel,
uses the voice channel's SSRC (on timeslot 1 of a Phase 2 channel, that plus
one). The frame stream (\-F) has the same SSRC,
timestamps and marker bits, one packet per vocoder frame.
.PP
The status of both streams carries the voice frequency, a description such as
"P25 TG 2501 src 1118028 "ENGINE 5" NAC 443" (a unit call says "P25 unit",
a telephone interconnect call "P25 telephone" and the radio in it), and
radiod's signal power, noise density and filter edges for the voice channel.
pcmrecord puts the description in its messages, in each file's attributes and
in the $d of an \-\-exec command. monitor shows the stream's SSRC (for a group
call, the talkgroup), the frequency and, from the signal figures, the
carrier's SNR; it does not show the description, so it shows neither talker
nor alias. The SNR measures the radio signal, not the decoding.
.PP
Encrypted voice is not decoded: a transmission's voice is decoded only after
a header or encryption sync of that transmission has said it is unencrypted,
and on a trunked channel only when the call tracking agrees. Voice that
arrives before that is held, then sent in order or dropped. A fade shorter
than half a second does not end a transmission, so at a weak signal up to one
superframe (360 ms) of an encrypted transmission that began during the fade
can be decoded before its own encryption sync stops it.
.SH SIGNALS
SIGUSR1 increases verbosity; SIGUSR2 decreases it, as in radiod. SIGINT,
SIGTERM and SIGQUIT stop p25d, and its voice channels are released.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B "What has been tested."
Phase 1 was tested live on the air on a VHF L3Harris linear simulcast trunked
system at a weak signal, with only the control channel configured in radiod.
Phase 2 has not been tested live on the air: Phase 2 voice and the Phase 2
control channel have been tested only from a recording of a real Phase 2 voice
channel and from generated signals, both through a real radiod.
.TP
.B "The vocoder is optional, and not a complete one."
Speech decoding uses mbelib, which is optional when p25d is built. mbelib
decodes the vocoder parameters, enhances the spectral amplitudes and
synthesises voiced speech as the standards describe, but it takes shortcuts
(checked for IMBE in mbelib 1.3.0): unvoiced bands are made from a few
random-phase sinusoids and a little noise instead of shaped white noise, which
is the audible difference; the standard's adaptive smoothing at high error
rates is absent; silence frames are not treated as such; and it uses the C
library's rand(), so its output is not repeatable bit for bit. p25d applies
the standard's frame repeat and mute rules itself. The vocoder is called
through a small interface, so a complete implementation can take its place,
and \-F gives the frames to a vocoder of your own. The author has one that
follows the specification more closely, but it is not yet packaged for
distribution. mbelib's README says it is
for educational purposes only and that the algorithms may be covered by
patents, so packagers may prefer to build without it.
.TP
.B "At a weak signal a damaged word can pass its error correction."
On the air about 0.7% of the encryption syncs that decoded carried values the
system never sends; a few of those, and two voice headers, said unencrypted.
The Reed-Solomon stage accepts whatever it can complete, erasures included, as
the codes allow. The effects are a wrong line in the log, now and then a call
record with no voice, and a moment of an encrypted transmission decoded as
noise, or of a clear one held back, until the next sync. This is left so on
purpose, to keep the rule simple and the decoder as sensitive as the codes
permit.
.TP
.B "More than one system."
A trunked group call's stream has the talkgroup number as its SSRC, and
talkgroup numbers are unique only within one system. Run one p25d, with its
own output group, per system.
.TP
.B "Rotation needs every possible control channel configured, for now."
See RADIOD CONFIGURATION. A later version is to ask radiod for the site's
announced secondary control channels when the control channel goes silent,
so that only one need be configured.
.TP
.B "One front end per system, for now."
radiod serves one front end per instance, and p25d asks for voice channels
from the radiod that sends the control channel, so the control channel and
every voice channel it grants must lie within that front end's coverage; a
call on a frequency outside it is logged and not followed. A system whose
channels span more than one tuner needs p25d to work with more than one
radiod at once, asking for each channel from the radiod whose front end
covers it. That is planned, not yet done.
.TP
.B "A composite control channel is not handled."
On some sites, typically small ones, the control channel can itself be given
to a call as its voice channel: while the call lasts the frequency carries
voice instead of trunking messages, and becomes the control channel again
afterwards. p25d logs such a call but does not decode it (the call ends with
no voice received); it takes the voice as the control channel having been
lost, and finds it again when trunking messages return.
.TP
.B "Other known limits."
The deny list (\-x) tests only a call's own talkgroup, while the allow list
(\-g) tests every member of a patch. Two calls on one frequency from two
systems heard at once give the second one no voice.
.TP
.B "The frame stream's numbers are p25d's own."
RTP payload type 97 and encoding value 12 are not in ka9q-radio's rtp.h. No
other ka9q-radio program reads the stream, and the numbers may change if the
package assigns them.
.TP
.B "TTL and [global]."
radiod makes a voice channel asked for on demand from its [global] section:
"data =" must be set there, and "ttl =" there is the TTL of those channels,
which a request cannot change. With "ttl = 0" a p25d on another host sees the
control channel but none of the voice channels. p25d's own voice and frame
output has TTL 0 unless \-T says otherwise.
.TP
.B "A radiod defect this runs into."
A channel created by a command that also changes its sample rate from the
template's sends its first packets with the template's payload type, and the
first data of the channel's own type leaves radiod just before the status that
names the type. p25d takes or drops each of those first packets according to
what it knows of its payload type when it arrives, and asks radiod to correct
the type. This is a few tens of milliseconds at the start of a new channel,
before any voice; decoding is not affected.
.SH CREDITS
sdrtrunk (Dennis Sheirer, GPL-3) and OP25 (Max H. Parke KA1RBI, Graham J.
Norbury and others, GPL-3) were both used as references for P25 implementation
details, along with the TIA-102 documents. Taken from them, with a credit
beside each in the source: the lengths of the Phase 2 MAC structures, the
field positions of the Phase 2 multi-fragment messages, the Motorola talker
alias table and the rule for an all-zero encryption sync. GNU Radio (GPL-3)
was used to investigate the signal processing chain; no GNU Radio code is in
this tree.
Speech decoding uses mbelib (https://github.com/szechyjs/mbelib) when it is
installed.
.PP
Much of what is here was first worked out, prototyped and run in production by
the author in the
receivers\ behind\ scanrad.io.
That is especially true of the front-end signal processing: the approach to
receive filtering, carrier frequency tracking, symbol timing recovery and
frame sync was originally written for those receivers and tuned there.
Those receivers are not ka9q-radio code: what is here is that work, rewritten
by the author in plain C for this tree with the assistance of Anthropic's
Claude (the Fable and Opus models).
.SH FILES
.TP
.B p25d@.service
systemd template unit; the instance name is the input group. Its output is
appended to /var/log/p25d.log.
.TP
.B config/examples/fragments/p25.conf
Example radiod section.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode a group, follow its calls and send the speech of clear calls:
.PP
.nf
p25d \-R p25\-pcm.local p25\-iq.local
.fi
.PP
The same, with the vocoder frames as well, to listeners on the local network:
.PP
.nf
p25d \-T 1 \-R p25\-pcm.local \-F p25\-frames.local p25\-iq.local
.fi
.PP
As a service:
.PP
.nf
systemctl enable \-\-now p25d@p25\-iq.local
.fi
